{
  "dom_id": "questionContainer",
  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14215763/assets/d4bcdb6515b262c070c7ed689263efbbde8a350ad6a28b4fc106de281e69ff46.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2t,\\,y=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$",
  "worked_solution": "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a positive number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nTo solve the above integral, we use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & ⇒ & v=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =2t(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-2(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)+2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =-2\\pi (-1) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
